  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14165395/assets/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
